Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "british-tank-lineage": britishTankArticle({
    title:"British Tank Lineage",
    eyebrow:"British armoured development · 1943–1985",
    infoboxKicker:"Universal-tank system",
    infoboxTitle:"British tank lineage",
    lead:"Britain's postwar tank lineage develops from the wartime Cromwell and Comet through the <a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurion</a> universal tank, the limited <a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror</a> heavy-support force, the <a href='chieftain-tank.html'>Chieftain</a> main battle tank, and the small early <a href='challenger-1.html'>Challenger 1</a> fleet. Strong rifled guns and prepared-position fighting remain characteristic, while Commonwealth geography imposes strict transport, recovery, and expeditionary limits.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-l7c3w writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T21:10:00-06:00 -->The fourteen registered service configurations, introduction dates, weights, crews, guns, engines, road speeds, universal-tank doctrine, Conway's wartime experimental status, prototype-only Churchill Crocodile, Charioteer and Caernarvon boundaries, Conqueror integral-infrared requirement, FV4202 test status, accelerated Chieftain reliability effort, Mk 8 and Mk 10 improvements, Challenger 1's 1984 status, small fleet, British and Canadian support concentration, and absence from the Falklands are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Conway's exact 1946–47 placement and technical reconstruction are extrapolative; prototype totals, production totals, armor values, dimensions, ammunition loads, unit establishments, export history beyond the broad Charioteer role, complete variant sequences, losses, and post-1985 development remain open unless separately established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"doctrine",title:"Universal-tank doctrine",html:"<p>Britain preserves the idea that one balanced tank should defeat enemy armor, support infantry, and exploit mobility rather than divide the army permanently into cruiser and infantry types. The <a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurion</a> becomes the enduring expression of that approach.</p><p>The doctrine does not remove specialist vehicles. <a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror</a> supplies limited long-range heavy support, while <a href='charioteer-tank.html'>Charioteer</a> supplies a reserve and export stopgap.</p>"},
      {id:"registry",title:"Registered sequence",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Vehicle</th><th>Introduction</th><th>Weight</th><th>Main armament</th><th>Road speed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cromwell Mk IV</td><td>1943</td><td>28.0 t</td><td>75 mm ROQF Mk V</td><td>64 km/h</td></tr><tr><td>Comet</td><td>1944</td><td>32.5 t</td><td>76.2 mm 77 mm HV</td><td>51 km/h</td></tr><tr><td><a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurion Mk 1 / 2 / 3</a></td><td>1945 / 1946 / 1947</td><td>42.0–50.8 t</td><td>17-pounder / 20-pounder</td><td>35 km/h</td></tr><tr><td><a href='charioteer-tank.html'>Charioteer</a></td><td>1952</td><td>28.5 t</td><td>83.4 mm 20-pounder</td><td>51 km/h</td></tr><tr><td><a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror Mk 2</a></td><td>1956</td><td>66.0 t</td><td>120 mm L1 L/55</td><td>34 km/h</td></tr><tr><td><a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurion Mk 10 / 13</a></td><td>1959 / 1962</td><td>52.0–52.5 t</td><td>105 mm L7 L/52</td><td>35 km/h</td></tr><tr><td><a href='chieftain-tank.html'>Chieftain Mk 3 / 5 / 8 / 10</a></td><td>1966–1982</td><td>54.0–57.0 t</td><td>120 mm L11 L/55</td><td>40–45 km/h</td></tr><tr><td><a href='challenger-1.html'>Challenger 1 Mk 1</a></td><td>1984</td><td>62.0 t</td><td>120 mm L11A5 L/55</td><td>56 km/h</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"wartime",title:"Cromwell and Comet",html:"<p>Cromwell Mk IV enters service in 1943 as a fast 28-tonne cruiser. Comet follows in 1944 with the 76.2 mm 77 mm HV gun and becomes the immediate wartime bridge to Centurion.</p><p>The extended European war gives both vehicles a longer combat setting than their historical counterparts. Exact setting-specific campaigns, unit strengths, losses, and rebuilds remain outside the present register.</p>"},
      {id:"menagerie",title:"Wartime development branches",html:"<p>The wartime program includes cruiser, infantry, Commonwealth, tank-destroyer, antiaircraft, engineering, and experimental heavy-gun branches. Valentine, Matilda, Churchill, Excelsior, Ram, <a href='australian-cruiser-tank-program.html'>Australian Cruiser</a>, Archer, Achilles, Challenger, Avenger, Firefly, Tetrarch, Crusader AA, Skink, and South African armoured-car programs coexist with the Cromwell–Comet–Centurion sequence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><p>The <a href='churchill-crocodile.html'>Churchill Crocodile</a> remains a prototype after fuel-use and logistic concerns prevent battlefield adoption. The vehicle therefore belongs to the experimental record, not to the list of wartime service flame tanks.</p>"},
      {id:"centurion",title:"Centurion universal tank",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-p3d7k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T18:54:00-06:00 --><p><a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurion</a> Mk 1 enters service in 1945 and reaches Egypt, where it fights late Panther and scarce Tiger II vehicles.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The desert-corrected 17-pounder Mk 2 follows in 1946, and the 20-pounder Mk 3 is formally accepted in 1947 after Britain has lost its intended theater.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The later Mk 10 and Mk 13 adopt the 105 mm L7 while the sequence retains a four-person crew and 35 km/h registered road speed.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"conway",title:"Conway experimental branch",html:"<p>The wartime <a href='conway-experimental-tank.html'>Conway</a> branch is an experimental part of the Centurion development cycle.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1946–47 placement, heavy antitank gun on the A41 base, British wartime gun ancestry, and detailed account of turret, ammunition, loading, recoil, balance, transport, and recovery penalties form a compatible reconstruction of why the experiment matters.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"branches",title:"Stopgap and heavy-support branches",html:"<p><a href='charioteer-tank.html'>Charioteer</a> mounts a 20-pounder on the Cromwell automotive base as a reserve and export stopgap. It is not a heavy tank or a developmental ancestor of Chieftain.</p><p>The small <a href='caernarvon-development-vehicle.html'>Caernarvon</a> fleet provides training and development between Centurion and <a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror Mk 2</a> rather than forming a separate service-tank generation. Caernarvon retains modular infrared equipment; the 1956 Conqueror makes integral active-infrared night fighting a factory requirement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"chieftain",title:"Chieftain generation",html:"<p><a href='chieftain-tank.html'>Chieftain</a> reunifies heavy firepower and protection with the universal-tank role from 1966. Britain improves its engine reliability earlier than in historical development because German armor is an immediate strategic threat.</p><p>Mk 8 adds laser ranging and an improved automotive package in 1978. Mk 10 adds composite or applique turret protection, passive night equipment, and improved analog-digital fire control in 1982.</p>"},
      {id:"challenger",title:"Challenger transition",html:"<p><a href='challenger-1.html'>Challenger 1 Mk 1</a> enters service in 1984 with a 1,200 hp engine, 120 mm L11A5 rifled gun, and composite armor. The small fleet's support is concentrated in Britain and Canada.</p><p>The Army does not send Challenger to the Falklands. Recovery difficulty and the risk to the newest sixty-two-ton tank keep it out of the South Atlantic theater.</p>"},
      {id:"falklands",title:"Falklands force boundary",html:"<p>Eighteen <a href='chieftain-tank.html'>Chieftain Mk 10</a> tanks form the registered British theater strength. They fight with carriers, reconnaissance vehicles, artillery, engineers, and recovery teams against Argentine TAM-74A2 and Panzer XI formations.</p><p>Challenger remains in Britain and Canada; Warrior remains in troop trials. The campaign is therefore a contest between mature upgraded systems rather than the newest parade-ground vehicles.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"Universal-tank family",label:"Centurion"},{href:"conway-experimental-tank.html",kicker:"Wartime heavy-gun experiment",label:"Conway"},{href:"churchill-crocodile.html",kicker:"Prototype specialist branch",label:"Churchill Crocodile"},{href:"caernarvon-development-vehicle.html",kicker:"Heavy-family development bridge",label:"Caernarvon"},{href:"charioteer-tank.html",kicker:"Reserve and export stopgap",label:"Charioteer"},{href:"conqueror-tank.html",kicker:"Heavy-support branch",label:"Conqueror"},{href:"chieftain-tank.html",kicker:"Principal 1985 tank",label:"Chieftain"},{href:"challenger-1.html",kicker:"1984 successor",label:"Challenger 1"},{href:"battle-mount-pleasant-1985.html",kicker:"Principal campaign battle",label:"Battle of Mount Pleasant"}],
    facts:[["Country","United Kingdom"],["Coverage","1943–1985"],["Registered service configurations","Fourteen"],["Experimental heavy-gun branch","Conway · wartime; exact dates open"],["Prototype-only specialist","Churchill Crocodile"],["Core doctrine","Universal tank"],["Principal gun tradition","Rifled tank guns"],["1985 expeditionary tank","Chieftain Mk 10"],["Newest service tank","Challenger 1 Mk 1"],["Challenger in Falklands","No"]]
  }),

  "centurion-tank": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Centurion",
    eyebrow:"British universal tank · service from 1945",
    infoboxKicker:"British universal-tank family",
    infoboxTitle:"Centurion",
    lead:"Centurion is the British universal-tank family introduced in 1945. Across the registered Mk 1, Mk 2, Mk 3, Mk 10, and Mk 13 sequence, its main armament develops from the 17-pounder through the 20-pounder to the 105 mm L7 while the vehicle becomes the foundation of postwar British armored doctrine.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-m4h8y writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T21:10:00-06:00 -->The five registered marks, introduction dates, weights, four-person crew, guns, engines, 35 km/h road speed, universal-tank role, Mk 1 combat in Egypt, Mk 2's desert correction, Mk 3's predeployment acceptance, modular night-fighting preparation, Conway's wartime experimental relationship, relationship to Conqueror and Chieftain, and Caernarvon and FV4202 boundaries are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Conway's exact 1946–47 placement, configuration, gun ancestry, and A41-limit interpretation are extrapolative; armor values, dimensions, ammunition loads, production totals, detailed subvariants, export customers, combat history beyond the registered 1945 Egyptian service, unit establishments, and retirement remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"development",title:"Development",html:"<p>Centurion emerges from British wartime experience seeking one vehicle with sufficient firepower, protection, and mobility for tank combat and infantry support. It replaces the older permanent cruiser-versus-infantry division with the universal-tank concept.</p>"},
      {id:"marks",title:"Registered marks",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Introduction</th><th>Weight</th><th>Main armament</th><th>Engine</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mk 1</td><td>1945</td><td>42.0 t</td><td>76.2 mm 17-pounder</td><td>600 hp</td></tr><tr><td>Mk 2</td><td>1946</td><td>47.0 t</td><td>76.2 mm 17-pounder</td><td>650 hp</td></tr><tr><td>Mk 3</td><td>1947</td><td>50.8 t</td><td>83.4 mm 20-pounder</td><td>650 hp</td></tr><tr><td>Mk 10</td><td>1959</td><td>52.0 t</td><td>105 mm L7 L/52</td><td>650 hp</td></tr><tr><td>Mk 13</td><td>1962</td><td>52.5 t</td><td>105 mm L7 L/52</td><td>650 hp</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"armament",title:"Armament development",html:"<p>The early 17-pounder marks lead to the 83.4 mm 20-pounder Mk 3 and finally the 105 mm L7 Mk 10 and Mk 13. This gun-growth capacity is central to Centurion's long service and helps make the separate Conqueror heavy-support role temporary.</p>"},
      {id:"mobility",title:"Mobility",html:"<p>All five registered marks have a 35 km/h road speed. Weight grows from 42.0 to 52.5 tonnes while engine output rises from 600 to 650 hp. Range, transmission changes, suspension detail, ground pressure, and bridge classification remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"branches",title:"Related developments",html:"<p>The wartime <a href='conway-experimental-tank.html'>Conway</a> experiment belongs to the Centurion development cycle; its exact date, gun and interpretation as the practical A41 growth limit remain incorporated reconstruction. The small <a href='caernarvon-development-vehicle.html'>Caernarvon</a> fleet is a training and development bridge between Centurion and <a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror</a>, not a separate operational lineage. FV4202 is a three-vehicle test program whose work feeds <a href='chieftain-tank.html'>Chieftain</a>; it never becomes a service tank.</p>"},
      {id:"service",title:"Wartime and Commonwealth service",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-r8m2v writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T18:54:00-06:00 --><p>Centurion Mk 1 reaches Egypt in 1945 and fights late Panther and scarce Tiger II vehicles. Mk 2 is the 1946 desert-corrected model, emphasizing observation, faster target acquisition, improved controls, and standardized preparation for modular night-fighting equipment.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><p>Mk 3 is formally accepted in 1947 with the 20-pounder and mature wartime optical and electrical package, but loses its intended theater before operational deployment. The later claim that it was designed specifically to defeat Panzer IX is an anachronism: British procurement first sees the complete Panzer IX at the 1947 Siegestag parade.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Later national fleets, exports, conversions, and retirement remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>Centurion establishes the British preference for a four-person tank with a strong rifled gun and the ability to accept successive upgrades. Chieftain succeeds it in first-line service while preserving the universal-tank principle.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"National development sequence",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"conway-experimental-tank.html",kicker:"Wartime heavy-gun experiment",label:"Conway"},{href:"caernarvon-development-vehicle.html",kicker:"Postwar development bridge",label:"Caernarvon"},{href:"charioteer-tank.html",kicker:"Cromwell-based stopgap",label:"Charioteer"},{href:"conqueror-tank.html",kicker:"Heavy-support companion",label:"Conqueror"},{href:"chieftain-tank.html",kicker:"Universal-tank successor",label:"Chieftain"},{href:"commonwealth.html",kicker:"Strategic system",label:"British Commonwealth"},{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Capability portal",label:"Military Technology"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Entered service","1945"],["Registered marks","Mk 1, 2, 3, 10, and 13"],["Crew","4"],["Combat weight","42.0–52.5 t"],["Main guns","17-pounder; 20-pounder; 105 mm L7"],["Road speed","35 km/h"],["Doctrine","Universal tank"]]
  }),

  "charioteer-tank": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Charioteer",
    eyebrow:"British reserve and export tank · introduced 1952",
    infoboxKicker:"Cromwell-based gun-tank conversion",
    infoboxTitle:"Charioteer",
    lead:"Charioteer is a British reserve and export stopgap introduced in 1952 by mounting the 83.4 mm 20-pounder gun on the Cromwell automotive base. It supplies additional postwar firepower while Centurion production expands, but it is neither a heavy tank nor an ancestor of Chieftain.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-l4y8f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T20:15:00-06:00 -->The designation, 1952 introduction, 28.5-tonne combat weight, four-person crew, 83.4 mm 20-pounder, 600 hp engine, 51 km/h road speed, Cromwell basis, reserve and export stopgap role, and exclusion from the Chieftain lineage are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Conversion total, turret design details, armor, ammunition load, manufacturers, export customers in the altered setting, units, combat record, and retirement remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"purpose",title:"Purpose",html:"<p>Britain needs more postwar gun power before new <a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurions</a> are available in sufficient numbers. Charioteer converts existing Cromwell automotive capacity into a 20-pounder carrier for reserve and export service.</p>"},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Registered configuration",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><tbody><tr><th>Introduction</th><td>1952</td></tr><tr><th>Combat weight</th><td>28.5 tonnes</td></tr><tr><th>Crew</th><td>Four</td></tr><tr><th>Main armament</th><td>83.4 mm 20-pounder</td></tr><tr><th>Engine</th><td>600 hp</td></tr><tr><th>Road speed</th><td>51 km/h</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"mobility",title:"Mobility",html:"<p>The 600 hp engine gives approximately 21.1 hp per tonne and supports a 51 km/h registered road speed. Range, suspension rebuilds, transmission, ground pressure, and cross-country speed remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"role",title:"Reserve and export service",html:"<p>Charioteer is assigned to reserve and export roles rather than the first-line universal-tank force. Exact British formations, Commonwealth recipients, foreign customers, and service dates in this setting remain unregistered.</p>"},
      {id:"boundary",title:"Lineage boundary",html:"<p>The vehicle is not a heavy tank and does not lead technically to Chieftain. It is a practical conversion branch beside Centurion, while <a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror</a> occupies the genuine heavy-support role.</p>"},
      {id:"documentation",title:"Documented limits",html:"<p>Armor, dimensions, ammunition stowage, sights, radios, conversion works, procurement totals, exports, losses, and withdrawal remain open. Historical export lists should not be transferred automatically across the setting's altered postwar alliances.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"National development sequence",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"First-line counterpart",label:"Centurion"},{href:"conqueror-tank.html",kicker:"Heavy-support branch",label:"Conqueror"},{href:"chieftain-tank.html",kicker:"Not a direct successor",label:"Chieftain"},{href:"commonwealth.html",kicker:"Strategic system",label:"British Commonwealth"},{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Capability portal",label:"Military Technology"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Introduction","1952"],["Type","Reserve and export gun tank"],["Combat weight","28.5 t"],["Crew","4"],["Main armament","83.4 mm 20-pounder"],["Engine","600 hp"],["Road speed","51 km/h"],["Chieftain ancestor","No"]]
  }),

  "conway-experimental-tank": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Conway experimental tank",
    eyebrow:"British heavy-gun experiment · wartime program",
    infoboxKicker:"Centurion-based experimental gun tank",
    infoboxTitle:"Conway",
    lead:"Conway was a British wartime experiment within the pressured Centurion development cycle rather than a registered service-tank generation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Placing a heavy antitank weapon on the A41 automotive base and reading the experiment as proof of Centurion's practical growth limit are compatible reconstructions of that authorial premise; the exact 1946–47 placement and technical chain are not independently fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-g3v7m writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T21:10:00-06:00 -->Conway's existence as an experimental wartime branch within Centurion development and its absence from the service-tank lineage are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1946–47 dating, precise A41 configuration, heavy-antitank purpose, British wartime gun ancestry, detailed trial narrative, and interpretation that it demonstrated the chassis's practical upper limit are incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Gun designation and caliber, prototype count, turret configuration, combat weight, crew, engine changes, armor, dimensions, ammunition load, firing trials, range, sights, contractors, disposition, and relationship to later ordnance projects remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"requirement",title:"Requirement",html:"<p>British crews in Egypt encountered increasingly powerful German and Italian guns while procurement staffs anticipated further Panther- and Tiger-class development. Conway examined a direct response: retain the new A41 chassis and install a weapon intended to defeat the next heavy tank at ranges where the 17-pounder was becoming uncomfortable.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"gun",title:"British heavy-gun ancestry",html:"<p>The setting's Conway weapon descends from British wartime heavy-ordnance experiments, including work derived from large antiaircraft-gun barrels. It is not the historical postwar L1 copied backward into 1946, and the surviving record does not yet fix its caliber or service-style designation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Experimental configuration",html:"<p>The program uses the Centurion automotive base and a substantially enlarged turret or fighting compartment for the heavy weapon. Installation demonstrates that the chassis can physically carry and fire the gun. Exact hull mark, turret construction, recoil system, crew arrangement, engine output, suspension changes, and test weight remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"trials",title:"Trials",html:"<p>Firing and automotive trials expose the difference between carrying a weapon and fielding a balanced tank. Turret mass affects bearings, suspension and hull balance. Large projectiles and propellant increase loader fatigue while reducing ammunition stowage. Recoil loads require reinforcement, and the complete vehicle becomes harder to transport and recover.</p>"},
      {id:"centurion",title:"Relationship to Centurion Mk 3",html:"<p>Conway investigates the heavy-tank solution while <a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurion Mk 3</a> pursues a more powerful 20-pounder within the ordinary universal-tank envelope. Mk 3 is accepted in 1947. It preserves a four-person, general-purpose tank rather than reproducing Conway's specialist heavy-gun installation.</p>"},
      {id:"result",title:"Program result",html:"<p>Conway does not fail because its gun cannot operate. It succeeds as an experiment by establishing that continued up-gunning would erode the combination of firepower, protection, mobility, ammunition endurance, transportability and maintainability for which Centurion had been selected. No Conway service generation is registered.</p>"},
      {id:"succession",title:"Heavy-family succession",html:"<p>The heavy-gun requirement moves into the A45/FV200 family. The <a href='caernarvon-development-vehicle.html'>Caernarvon</a> development fleet supplies heavy-chassis experience before the 1956 <a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror Mk 2</a> combines the 120 mm L1 with a purpose-built heavy-support vehicle.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Open technical record",html:"<p>Prototype identities, drawings, trial establishments, firing dates, ammunition, performance, failures, range results, recovery exercises, storage locations and disposal remain open. No exact historical Conway specification should be transferred automatically to this earlier British-derived program.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"National development sequence",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"Automotive basis",label:"Centurion"},{href:"caernarvon-development-vehicle.html",kicker:"Later heavy-family bridge",label:"Caernarvon"},{href:"conqueror-tank.html",kicker:"Operational heavy successor",label:"Conqueror"},{href:"second-battle-of-britain.html",kicker:"Late-war strategic context",label:"Second Battle of Britain"},{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Capability portal",label:"Military Technology"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Program period","Wartime · exact dates open"],["Status","Experimental only"],["Automotive basis","Centurion A41 family · incorporated"],["Purpose","Heavy antitank gun trial · incorporated"],["Gun ancestry","British wartime heavy-ordnance program · incorporated"],["Interpretive result","Practical A41 growth limit"],["Exact gun and specifications","Open"]]
  }),

  "churchill-crocodile": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Churchill Crocodile",
    eyebrow:"British flame-tank prototype · wartime program",
    infoboxKicker:"Prototype Churchill specialist vehicle",
    infoboxTitle:"Churchill Crocodile",
    lead:"The Churchill Crocodile was a British wartime flame-tank project based on the Churchill infantry tank. Fuel-use concerns prevented meaningful battlefield employment and left it at prototype status rather than producing the operational assault force known from historical Allied service.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The familiar hull-projector and separate-trailer arrangement is retained as compatible design extrapolation, not a separately specified setting fact.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-h8q2p writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T21:10:00-06:00 -->The Churchill Crocodile's existence, Churchill basis, flame-weapon role, prototype-only status, lack of meaningful battlefield use, and failure to proceed because fuel-use concerns rapidly worsened are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The hull-projector and separate-trailer configuration is compatible incorporated design detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Prototype date, exact Churchill mark, number built, projector, fuel mixture, trailer design, fuel capacity, range, crew, retained gun armament, trials, unit ownership, contractors, and disposition remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"requirement",title:"Assault requirement",html:"<p>The Churchill family supplied heavily protected infantry tanks and several engineering or gun-carrier experiments. Crocodile investigated a mobile flame weapon for fortified positions, fieldworks and close assault while retaining a turreted tank's protected movement.</p>"},
      {id:"design",title:"Configuration",html:"<p>The prototype follows the recognizable Crocodile architecture: a Churchill hull carries the projector and tows a separate armored fuel trailer. Exact projector position, hose routing, couplings, trailer suspension, fuel composition, capacity, normal gun armament and Churchill mark are not fixed in the setting record.</p>"},
      {id:"trials",title:"Prototype trials",html:"<p>Trials demonstrated the concept sufficiently for fuel supply to become the controlling objection. A flame tank consumed a specialized bulk fuel load that had to be blended, transported, protected and transferred near the front. The trailer and its coupling added maneuver, maintenance and recovery requirements.</p>"},
      {id:"fuel",title:"Fuel and support burden",html:"<p>British procurement judged projected fuel use too costly for a specialist whose ammunition could be exhausted rapidly. The problem included tanker transport, depot storage, replenishment vehicles, fire risk and competition with the fuel demands of ordinary armored formations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"Cancellation before battlefield adoption",html:"<p>The Crocodile did not enter operational battlefield service. No assault regiment, campaign deployment or production fleet should be inferred from the historical vehicle's record. Surviving prototypes remained part of the Churchill development menagerie.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"churchill",title:"Place in the Churchill family",html:"<p>Churchill gun tanks, gun carriers, engineering vehicles and other assault adaptations continued through the extended war. Crocodile's cancellation applied to this flame-weapon branch; it did not end Churchill specialist-vehicle development as a whole.</p>"},
      {id:"record",title:"Open prototype record",html:"<p>Design papers, establishments, prototype identities, trial grounds, dates, range figures, fuel volumes, crew reports and vehicle disposition remain open. Historical Crocodile production and campaign totals do not apply.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"Wider development menagerie",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"Wartime universal-tank program",label:"Centurion"},{href:"conway-experimental-tank.html",kicker:"Contemporary experimental branch",label:"Conway"},{href:"british-artillery.html",kicker:"British battlefield fire system",label:"British Artillery"},{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Capability portal",label:"Military Technology"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Type","Prototype flame tank"],["Chassis family","Churchill"],["Working fuel arrangement","Separate towed trailer"],["Status","Prototype only"],["Meaningful battlefield use","None"],["Reason not adopted","Fuel-use concerns"],["Exact date and specifications","Open"]]
  }),

  "caernarvon-development-vehicle": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Caernarvon development vehicle",
    eyebrow:"British heavy-chassis development and training vehicle · before 1956",
    infoboxKicker:"Centurion–Conqueror development bridge",
    infoboxTitle:"Caernarvon",
    lead:"Caernarvon was Britain's very small, deliberately transitional development and training bridge between Centurion and Conqueror. It investigated integral infrared but retained an external or modular night-fighting package and never became a separate service-tank generation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The A45/FV200 chassis and Centurion-turret reconstruction, together with the detailed crew, workshop, transport, and recovery program, are compatible extrapolations from that role.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-j5m9r writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T21:10:00-06:00 -->Caernarvon's development and training role, position between Centurion and Conqueror, very small fleet, transitional status, investigation of integral infrared, retention of an external or modular night-fighting package, and exclusion from the service-tank lineage are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its A45/FV200 chassis and Centurion-turret configuration, detailed training functions, and organizational consequences are compatible incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact marks, dates, number built, turret, gun, combat weight, engine, armor, dimensions, speed, ammunition, units, trials, and final disposition remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"purpose",title:"Purpose",html:"<p>Britain's heavy-gun program required experience with the new chassis before the final Conqueror turret and weapon system were ready. Caernarvon combined a familiar Centurion-derived turret and gun installation with the heavy-family hull so automotive and support trials could proceed.</p>"},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Development configuration",html:"<p>The development vehicle uses the A45/FV200-derived heavy chassis and a known Centurion-family turret installation. Exact turret mark, 20-pounder configuration, hull standard, armor, engine, suspension, mass and performance remain open. The arrangement is a test and training expedient rather than an alternative universal tank.</p>"},
      {id:"fleet",title:"Small fleet",html:"<p>Only a very small number entered the development establishment. The record does not fix a production total, but the vehicles served in the category of prototypes and training machines rather than a field force of hundreds.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"training",title:"Training and support trials",html:"<p>Caernarvon allowed evaluation of transmission behavior, suspension loads, fuel consumption, bridge limits, transporters, recovery requirements, workshop access and crew handling on the heavier chassis. These tasks could proceed without waiting for the complete 120 mm fighting compartment.</p>"},
      {id:"infrared",title:"Modular night-fighting equipment",html:"<p>Procurement examined integral infrared equipment but retained an external or modular package because Caernarvon was transitional. The hull could support night trials without committing the full fleet to a factory-integrated driver, gunner and commander installation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"conqueror",title:"Transition to Conqueror",html:"<p><a href='conqueror-tank.html'>Conqueror Mk 2</a> entered service in 1956 with the 120 mm L1 and integral active-infrared night-fighting provision. Caernarvon supplied automotive and organizational experience; it did not remain alongside Conqueror as a separate operational mark.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Open development record",html:"<p>Vehicle numbers, serials, marks, establishments, trial stations, workshop reports, road and cross-country performance, bridge classifications, infrared set design and withdrawal remain unregistered.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"National development sequence",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"Turret and training ancestry",label:"Centurion"},{href:"conway-experimental-tank.html",kicker:"Earlier heavy-gun experiment",label:"Conway"},{href:"conqueror-tank.html",kicker:"Operational successor",label:"Conqueror"},{href:"chieftain-tank.html",kicker:"Later role consolidation",label:"Chieftain"},{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Capability portal",label:"Military Technology"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Period","Before 1956 · exact dates open"],["Type","Development and training vehicle"],["Working chassis lineage","A45/FV200 heavy family"],["Working turret lineage","Centurion-derived"],["Fleet size","Very small · exact number open"],["Night equipment","External or modular active infrared"],["Service-tank generation","No"],["Successor","Conqueror Mk 2"]]
  }),

  "conqueror-tank": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Conqueror",
    eyebrow:"British heavy gun tank · introduced 1956",
    infoboxKicker:"Limited heavy-support tank",
    infoboxTitle:"Conqueror Mk 2",
    lead:"Conqueror Mk 2 is Britain's limited heavy-support tank introduced in 1956. Its 120 mm L1 L/55 gun supplies the heavy-firepower complement to Centurion's universal-tank role, and active-infrared night fighting becomes a factory requirement after Caernarvon retained an external or modular package.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-k2v6s writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T21:10:00-06:00 -->The Conqueror Mk 2 designation, 1956 introduction, 66.0-tonne combat weight, four-person crew, 120 mm L1 L/55 gun, 810 hp engine, 34 km/h road speed, heavy-support relationship to Centurion, Caernarvon development boundary, and integral active-infrared night-fighting requirement are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Production total, armor, dimensions, ammunition load, exact infrared set, fire-control details, formations, campaign history, retirement, and calculated power-to-weight figure remain open or extrapolative in the altered setting.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"requirement",title:"Heavy-support requirement",html:"<p>Britain develops Conqueror to engage heavy enemy armor beyond the comfortable reach of early <a href='centurion-tank.html'>Centurion</a> marks. In service it supports the universal-tank fleet rather than replacing it.</p>"},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Registered configuration",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><tbody><tr><th>Mark</th><td>Conqueror Mk 2</td></tr><tr><th>Introduction</th><td>1956</td></tr><tr><th>Combat weight</th><td>66.0 tonnes</td></tr><tr><th>Crew</th><td>Four</td></tr><tr><th>Main armament</th><td>120 mm L1 L/55</td></tr><tr><th>Engine</th><td>810 hp</td></tr><tr><th>Road speed</th><td>34 km/h</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"firepower",title:"Firepower",html:"<p>The 120 mm L1 gives the heavy-support force a distinct long-range weapon. Ammunition types, carried load, rangefinding system, stabilization, rate of fire, and setting-specific penetration remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"night",title:"Integral night-fighting system",html:"<p>Active infrared is part of the factory requirement rather than an accessory issued to selected vehicles after production. Generating capacity, protected wiring, controls, and driver, gunner, and commander equipment are designed around night operation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><p>The register does not fix illuminator placement, sight models, effective range, field doctrine, production blocks, or whether every training vehicle carried the complete operational set at all times.</p>"},
      {id:"mobility",title:"Mobility and sustainment",html:"<p>At 66 tonnes, Conqueror imposes bridge, transport, recovery, and maintenance demands beyond Centurion. Its 810 hp engine produces approximately 12.3 hp per tonne and a registered 34 km/h road speed.</p>"},
      {id:"caernarvon",title:"Caernarvon bridge",html:"<p>The small <a href='caernarvon-development-vehicle.html'>Caernarvon</a> fleet joins Centurion turret experience to the heavy-family chassis for training and development. Integral infrared is investigated but left as an external or modular package on the transitional vehicle. Caernarvon does not form a separate service-tank generation in the registry.</p>"},
      {id:"succession",title:"End of the separate heavy branch",html:"<p>The 105 mm Centurion improves universal-tank firepower, and <a href='chieftain-tank.html'>Chieftain</a> later combines a 120 mm gun with the principal fleet role. Exact Conqueror withdrawal and disposal dates remain unregistered.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"National development sequence",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"Supported universal tank",label:"Centurion"},{href:"conway-experimental-tank.html",kicker:"Wartime heavy-gun predecessor",label:"Conway"},{href:"caernarvon-development-vehicle.html",kicker:"Immediate development bridge",label:"Caernarvon"},{href:"chieftain-tank.html",kicker:"Role-consolidating successor",label:"Chieftain"},{href:"charioteer-tank.html",kicker:"Contemporary stopgap",label:"Charioteer"},{href:"commonwealth.html",kicker:"Strategic system",label:"British Commonwealth"},{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Capability portal",label:"Military Technology"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Introduction","1956"],["Type","Heavy gun tank"],["Combat weight","66.0 t"],["Crew","4"],["Main armament","120 mm L1 L/55"],["Engine","810 hp"],["Calculated power-to-weight","12.3 hp/t"],["Road speed","34 km/h"],["Night fighting","Integral active infrared"]]
  }),

  "chieftain-tank": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Chieftain",
    eyebrow:"British main battle tank · service from 1966",
    infoboxKicker:"British 120 mm universal tank",
    infoboxTitle:"Chieftain",
    lead:"Chieftain is Britain's principal 120 mm main battle tank from 1966. The registered Mk 3, Mk 5, Mk 8, and Mk 10 sequence combines the universal-tank role with a strong rifled gun, increasingly reliable automotive systems, laser ranging, passive night equipment, and improved protection and fire control.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-m7z2g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T20:15:00-06:00 -->The four registered marks, dates, weights, four-person crew, 120 mm L11 L/55 gun, engines, road speeds, accelerated reliability effort, Mk 8 laser rangefinder and automotive package, Mk 10 composite or applique turret protection, passive night equipment and analog-digital fire control, 1985 first-line role, eighteen-tank Falklands theater strength, and principal Mount Pleasant role are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Armor values, dimensions, ammunition load, detailed engine changes, sight models, production totals, units, losses, and postwar upgrades remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"development",title:"Development",html:"<p>Chieftain absorbs the heavy firepower requirement into the main universal-tank fleet. The three-vehicle FV4202 test program contributes development evidence but never enters service.</p><p>Germany's continuing armored strength makes reliability an immediate strategic requirement. Britain therefore improves the power pack earlier than in historical development.</p>"},
      {id:"marks",title:"Registered marks",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Introduction</th><th>Weight</th><th>Engine</th><th>Road speed</th><th>Registered change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mk 3</td><td>1966</td><td>54.0 t</td><td>650 hp</td><td>40 km/h</td><td>Initial registered service mark</td></tr><tr><td>Mk 5</td><td>1972</td><td>55.0 t</td><td>750 hp</td><td>43 km/h</td><td>Automotive development</td></tr><tr><td>Mk 8</td><td>1978</td><td>56.0 t</td><td>800 hp</td><td>45 km/h</td><td>Laser rangefinder; improved automotive package</td></tr><tr><td>Mk 10</td><td>1982</td><td>57.0 t</td><td>800 hp</td><td>45 km/h</td><td>Turret protection; passive night; improved fire control</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"armament",title:"Armament",html:"<p>All registered marks carry the 120 mm L11 L/55 rifled gun. Ammunition natures, carried load, loading drill, coaxial and commander's weapons, barrel life, and setting-specific penetration remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"fire-control",title:"Observation and fire control",html:"<p>Mk 8 introduces a laser rangefinder. Mk 10 adds passive night equipment and improved analog-digital fire control. Both sides in the Falklands possess first-line laser ranging, stabilization, passive night sights, and APFSDS, but neither possesses universal thermal hunter-killer systems.</p>"},
      {id:"protection",title:"Protection",html:"<p>Mk 10 adds composite or applique turret protection. Exact construction, coverage, thickness, mass distribution, and resistance remain unregistered. The article therefore records the protection class without importing historical Stillbrew details wholesale.</p>"},
      {id:"falklands",title:"Falklands War",html:"<p>Eighteen Chieftain Mk 10 tanks deploy to the South Atlantic. They form the heavy British armored component at <a href='battle-mount-pleasant-1985.html'>Mount Pleasant</a>, working with Cavalier, FV432, CVR(T), artillery, engineers, and recovery teams.</p><p>Chieftain holds the frontal-protection and 120 mm firepower advantage; Argentine TAM vehicles hold mobility, ground-pressure, and recovery advantages. Exact tank losses and individual actions remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"successor",title:"Challenger transition",html:"<p><a href='challenger-1.html'>Challenger 1</a> enters service in 1984 but remains a small, support-intensive fleet in Britain and Canada. Chieftain therefore remains the expeditionary first-line tank in 1985.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"National development sequence",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"Universal-tank predecessor",label:"Centurion"},{href:"conqueror-tank.html",kicker:"Heavy-gun predecessor",label:"Conqueror"},{href:"challenger-1.html",kicker:"1984 successor",label:"Challenger 1"},{href:"battle-mount-pleasant-1985.html",kicker:"Principal combat",label:"Battle of Mount Pleasant"},{href:"falklands-war.html",kicker:"Parent conflict",label:"Falklands–Malvinas War"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Entered service","1966"],["Registered marks","Mk 3, 5, 8, and 10"],["Crew","4"],["Combat weight","54.0–57.0 t"],["Main armament","120 mm L11 L/55"],["Engine output","650–800 hp"],["Road speed","40–45 km/h"],["Falklands variant","Mk 10"],["Falklands theater strength","18"]]
  }),

  "challenger-1": britishTankArticle({
    title:"Challenger 1",
    eyebrow:"British main battle tank · introduced 1984",
    infoboxKicker:"Early composite-armored successor",
    infoboxTitle:"Challenger 1 Mk 1",
    lead:"Challenger 1 Mk 1 is Britain's newest service tank in 1984. The 62-tonne four-person vehicle combines a 1,200 hp engine, 120 mm L11A5 rifled gun, and composite armor, but its small fleet and concentrated British–Canadian support base keep it out of the Falklands War.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-n3a6h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T20:15:00-06:00 -->The Challenger 1 Mk 1 designation, 1984 introduction, 62.0-tonne combat weight, four-person crew, 120 mm L11A5 L/55 gun, 1,200 hp engine, 56 km/h road speed, composite armor, small fleet, concentrated support in Britain and Canada, successor relationship to Chieftain, and nondeployment to the Falklands are fixed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Development origin, production total, armor composition and values, dimensions, ammunition load, fire-control details, units, Canadian support arrangements, later marks, and combat history remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"development",title:"Development",html:"<p>Challenger follows <a href='chieftain-tank.html'>Chieftain</a> as Britain's second postwar main-battle-tank generation. It retains a 120 mm rifled gun while adding composite armor and a substantial increase in automotive power.</p><p>The precise procurement path and relationship to historical export programs remain open in the setting registry.</p>"},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Registered configuration",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><tbody><tr><th>Mark</th><td>Challenger 1 Mk 1</td></tr><tr><th>Introduction</th><td>1984</td></tr><tr><th>Combat weight</th><td>62.0 tonnes</td></tr><tr><th>Crew</th><td>Four</td></tr><tr><th>Main armament</th><td>120 mm L11A5 L/55</td></tr><tr><th>Engine</th><td>1,200 hp</td></tr><tr><th>Road speed</th><td>56 km/h</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"protection",title:"Composite protection",html:"<p>Challenger is the first registered British service tank in the sequence with composite armor as an integral defining feature. Composition, thickness, coverage, mass, resistance, and repair procedures remain classified or unregistered.</p>"},
      {id:"mobility",title:"Mobility",html:"<p>The 1,200 hp engine supplies approximately 19.4 hp per tonne and a registered 56 km/h road speed. Range, transmission, suspension, ground pressure, bridge classification, and cross-country performance remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"fleet",title:"Early fleet",html:"<p>The 1984 fleet is small, and its specialized support is concentrated in Britain and Canada. Exact regiments, Canadian facilities, vehicle totals, spares, transporters, and readiness remain unregistered.</p>"},
      {id:"falklands",title:"Exclusion from the Falklands",html:"<p>The Army refuses to risk its newest sixty-two-ton tank in a difficult recovery theater. Challenger remains in Britain and Canada while eighteen Chieftain Mk 10s deploy.</p><p>Claims that Challenger fights at Mount Pleasant are superseded and incorrect.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"1985 status",html:"<p>Challenger has entered service but has not displaced Chieftain across the force. Later marks, upgrades, exports, combat history, and eventual replacement remain outside the present 1985 register.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"british-tank-lineage.html",kicker:"National development sequence",label:"British Tank Lineage"},{href:"chieftain-tank.html",kicker:"Principal predecessor",label:"Chieftain"},{href:"centurion-tank.html",kicker:"Universal-tank heritage",label:"Centurion"},{href:"commonwealth.html",kicker:"Strategic system",label:"British Commonwealth"},{href:"falklands-war.html",kicker:"War not entered",label:"Falklands–Malvinas War"},{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Capability portal",label:"Military Technology"}],
    facts:[["Country of origin","United Kingdom"],["Introduction","1984"],["Mark","Challenger 1 Mk 1"],["Combat weight","62.0 t"],["Crew","4"],["Main armament","120 mm L11A5 L/55"],["Engine","1,200 hp"],["Power-to-weight","19.4 hp/t"],["Road speed","56 km/h"],["Protection","Composite armor"],["Falklands service","None"]]
  })
});

function addBritishTankItem(slug, key, item) {
  const article = window.deepArticles[slug];
  if (!article) return;
  article[key] = article[key] || [];
  if (!article[key].some(existing => (item.id && existing.id === item.id) || (item.href && existing.href === item.href))) article[key].push(item);
}

